WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:05.900
So I said that today we would talk about a
contested or broker, democratic national convention.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:09.390
It is what it means, what the implications
are, what the math of it is.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:15.970
And as we get into that, I do think it's important
to understand that the reason a contested

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:24.029
convention is worth discussing is that in
practice it would most likely be used for

00:00:24.029 --> 00:00:29.109
the DNC to select an establishment centrist
candidate.

00:00:29.109 --> 00:00:34.190
Now it's, it's interesting to know about contested
conventions no matter what and how it works.

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:41.850
But in practice, I don't want to hide the
context being in 2020 that it is a mechanism

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:47.940
which could be used to deny a progressive
the nomination and allow democratic party

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:54.690
insiders to select whichever their centrist
of favorite is at the time that we finally

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:55.690
get to the convention.

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:59.739
So I don't want to hide that, but I don't
feel the need to bring it up, uh, throughout

00:00:59.739 --> 00:01:00.739
the entire story.

00:01:00.739 --> 00:01:02.480
So I just, I want to get that out of the way.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:07.860
So the way that the democratic nomination
works differently than the Republican nomination

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:14.900
is that to secure the nomination, you need
a majority of the delegates, meaning 50% plus

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:22.770
one or 1,991 1990 would be half the delegates.

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:25.460
You need 1991 to secure the nomination.

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:33.170
That's 50% plus one if nobody secures the
1990 plus one delegates from the primaries,

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:38.200
O w, w, which are then voted on in the first
round of the convention.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:41.250
Then you have what is called a brokered or
a contested convention.

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:45.150
There's a second round of voting, which happens
after negotiations.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:50.550
Often behind the scenes between candidates
with their delegates and something called

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:53.090
super delegates are allowed to vote.

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:57.360
Now, super delegates back in 2016 were allowed
to vote on the first round.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:02.930
This is why in 2016 you often had, you know,
Bernie and Hillary at different points with

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:08.399
roughly the same number of pledge delegates
secured via elections via primaries, but then

00:02:08.399 --> 00:02:10.399
you had plus superdelegates.

00:02:10.399 --> 00:02:15.299
The vast majority had expressed support for
Hillary Clinton.

00:02:15.299 --> 00:02:19.319
And that is why from a very early point, you
saw these numbers with a massively lopsided

00:02:19.319 --> 00:02:24.470
lead for Hillary Clinton in 2020 as of right
now, the superdelegates can't vote on the

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:25.470
first round.

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:31.580
Super delegates are widely known to support
establishment centrist candidates.

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:40.019
So it is likely, it's not guaranteed, but
it's likely that if nobody gets to 1991 delegates,

00:02:40.019 --> 00:02:44.819
adding the superdelegates for a second round
of voting would likely hand the convention

00:02:44.819 --> 00:02:46.749
to an establishment centrist.

00:02:46.749 --> 00:02:51.700
It's unlikely that if you allow super delegates
in, they would go overwhelmingly for Bernie

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:54.189
Sanders or for Elizabeth Warren.

00:02:54.189 --> 00:02:56.050
So now it's very early, right?

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:59.999
So why on earth would we now be talking about
a brokered convention?

00:02:59.999 --> 00:03:03.249
We've only had two primaries, Iowa and New
Hampshire.

00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:04.249
That's very true.

00:03:04.249 --> 00:03:11.890
But the primary so far combined with the polling
start to point us towards a contested or brokered

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:12.890
convention.

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:15.790
Again, unless something radically changes.

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:17.489
So this is about odds.

00:03:17.489 --> 00:03:22.120
There is no way to say right now we will or
we won't have a brokered convention.

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:23.569
There's not even a way to say that.

00:03:23.569 --> 00:03:26.730
We could be 80 or even 90% certain one way
or the other.

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:31.670
But with every passing day, the polling is
split amongst so many candidates with every

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:37.200
primary, where three, four, or five candidates
get some delegates with the addition of likely

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:41.529
delegates for Tom Stire in South Carolina
and Michael Bloomberg in some of the super

00:03:41.529 --> 00:03:46.000
Tuesday States, the odds of a brokered convention
continue to go up.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:51.549
And the really important element to understand
to all of this from a strategic perspective,

00:03:51.549 --> 00:03:54.900
is that this is asymmetrical.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:04:00.719
If the goal is to prevent a specific candidate
from securing the nomination, then you have

00:04:00.719 --> 00:04:06.950
an asymmetrical situation where Bernie needs
to win on the first round.

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:13.209
But anybody who doesn't want Bernie merely
needs to deny Bernie nine 1,991 delegates.

00:04:13.209 --> 00:04:15.730
I've used the football analogy before.

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:22.200
If you're winning by seven points, the off
fence needs a touchdown.

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:24.880
You only need to prevent a touchdown.

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:27.990
You can get them all the way down the field.

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:33.030
You can allow a field goal, you can do all
as long as you prevent the touchdown.

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:34.770
Um, you win.

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:36.820
And thus you have an asymmetrical situation.

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:38.800
It's very similar with Bernie Sanders.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:43.610
Bernie must get 50% plus one of the delegates
to get the nomination because on a second

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:45.880
round, he is very unlikely to do it.

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:52.940
The DNC insiders, the establishment merely
have to prevent Bernie from getting 50% plus

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:56.750
one that stacks things against Bernie Sanders
mathematically.

00:04:56.750 --> 00:04:57.750
Okay.

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:01.800
Case in point, look@thefivethirtyeight.com
estimates right now.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:07.910
As of right now, five 38 estimates that Bernie
Sanders has a 52% chance of winning the most

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:09.430
delegates before the convention.

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:15.610
A plurality of delegates, more delegates than
any other single candidate, but only a 36%

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:19.130
chance of winning more than half.

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:25.890
This would deny Bernie the nomination while
Bernie would still have a plurality of the

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:26.890
delegates.

00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:30.560
So now let's look at the math of this kind
of at the big picture level.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:37.120
We will use our pool of 3,980 pledge delegates,
39 80 remember you need half of that plus

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:44.070
one that's 1990 plus one if the race remains
roughly the way it looks right now from the

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:48.670
national polls through the primaries, this
is just our first estimate.

00:05:48.670 --> 00:05:53.940
You would expect, for example, Bernie Sanders
polling 23.6 nationally.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:57.710
That would be equivalent to roughly 939 delegates.

00:05:57.710 --> 00:06:04.930
Biden would get seven 65 Bloomberg five 65
Warren, four 94 and so on down.

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:11.390
Now in this scenario, Bernie gets the most
delegates a plurality, but it's not a majority

00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:13.500
and thus he doesn't get the nomination.

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:20.180
Now an astute viewer will notice this is too
crude an estimate because remember that most

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:23.440
primaries have this 15% viability threshold.

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:27.630
So if you don't get 15% in a particular primary,
you don't get any delegates.

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:32.830
So in New Hampshire, Elizabeth Warren got
9.2% of the vote, but she didn't get 9% of

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:33.830
the delegates.

00:06:33.830 --> 00:06:37.550
You got zero delegates because her share of
the vote didn't make her viable.

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:42.490
So over time, the voting in any one state
will consolidate.

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:46.840
In some States, candidates could end up with
a higher share of the delegates than their

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:47.840
vote total.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:54.540
For example, if you look at New Hampshire,
Bernie won 25.7% of the vote, but he got 37%

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:58.080
of the delegates because of that viability
threshold.

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:04.170
Amy Klobuchar got 20% of the vote in New Hampshire,
but she got six delegates.

00:07:04.170 --> 00:07:06.830
That's 25% of the delegates.

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:13.780
The non-viable candidates end up sort of subsidizing
a higher share of the delegates to the candidates

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:17.190
that do meet that 15% viability threshold.

00:07:17.190 --> 00:07:23.180
So this is a crude estimate, but I think you
get the idea with every primary we can fine

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:25.970
tune our mathematical expectations.

00:07:25.970 --> 00:07:32.020
In Iowa, nobody got more than 30% of the vote
and none of the delegate recipients dropped

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:33.020
out.

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:34.120
In New Hampshire.

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:38.880
No candidate got more than 26% of the vote
and none of the delegate recipients dropped

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:41.700
out or are likely to drop out anytime soon.

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:44.230
And remember that this is self reinforcing.

00:07:44.230 --> 00:07:50.230
The more it looks like nobody will get to
15 per 50% of the delegates, the more other

00:07:50.230 --> 00:07:55.520
candidates are incentivized to stay in to
see what can they bargain for when we get

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:56.710
to the convention.

00:07:56.710 --> 00:08:01.950
That possibility becomes more appealing to
other candidates when it doesn't seem like

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:04.620
anyone's going to get 50% plus one.

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:10.610
This could actually get significantly more
split than it is now making a broker convention

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:16.610
even more likely, because remember Tom Stiers
polling like 18 in a new poll in South Carolina,

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:21.440
he's likely to be another delegate recipient
that would again split the vote, the delegates

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:26.170
further Michael Bloomberg, despite the racist
audio that our friend Ben Dickson released

00:08:26.170 --> 00:08:30.950
is polling in the mid to high teens in Florida
and Texas, which would be another candidate

00:08:30.950 --> 00:08:31.950
getting delegates.

00:08:31.950 --> 00:08:39.840
So this isn't the math of how a brokered convention
even at this early stage seems to be developing

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:45.900
and it is asymmetrical warfare in that if
you deny Bernie the nomination, then it's

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:50.280
wide open for the establishment to pick whoever
they want in the second round of voting at

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:51.280
the convention.

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:52.690
So we'll of course be following it.

00:08:52.690 --> 00:08:58.160
We'll update the math with each passing primary,
but this is conceptually why it's already

00:08:58.160 --> 00:10:20.210
something that's

00:10:20.210 --> 00:10:55.510
being discussed.

